<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>SwarmExtensionSwarmUI-AudioLab</AssemblyName>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!-- Force the floating HartsyInference 'alpha.*' ref to resolve to the latest published version. -->
    <RestoreNoHttpCache>true</RestoreNoHttpCache>
    <!-- Engine source toggle (same pattern as SwarmUI-LLMAssistant): false = published NuGet;
         true = prebuilt local HartsyInference engine DLLs (local engine dev only — the HartsyRepo
         sibling checkout below doesn't exist for end users, so this MUST default false or every
         install without a local engine checkout fails with "no reference to HartsyInference"). -->
    <UseLocalHartsy Condition="'$(UseLocalHartsy)' == ''">false</UseLocalHartsy>
    <HartsyRepo Condition="'$(HartsyRepo)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../../../HartsyInference</HartsyRepo>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="../../SwarmUI.extension.props" />

  <!-- Inherit TargetFramework net8.0 from SwarmUI.extension.props (matches the net8.0 SwarmUI host). Do NOT
       override to net10.0: the HartsyInference engine is MULTI-TARGET (net8.0 + net10.0), so a net8.0 extension
       correctly resolves the net8.0 engine assets. A net10.0 extension pulls net10.0 engine DLLs that reference
       System.Runtime 10.0.0.0, which the net8.0 host's extension AssemblyLoadContext cannot resolve (roll-forward
       makes the RUNTIME net10 but not the net8.0 assembly-version resolution) → ReflectionTypeLoadException.
       CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies — copy the engine's private DLLs into our output so the per-extension
       AssemblyLoadContext can resolve them. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>true</CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FFMpegCore" Version="5.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Tests/ is its own project (own xunit/Test.Sdk references, net10.0) — without this, the SDK's default
       **/*.cs glob would also compile its sources into this net8.0 Web SDK assembly and fail. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Remove="Tests/**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- RELEASE: the published meta-package. Was temporarily split into 10 direct sub-package
       references (alpha.45) because the meta-package's own publish step didn't exist yet — it was
       a manual, easily-forgotten `dotnet pack`+push that had been stuck at alpha.44 for weeks. Fixed
       2026-07-09: "HartsyInference" is now packed via a real project (src/HartsyInference.Meta in
       the engine repo) wired into publish-nuget.yml, so it publishes automatically on every push
       like every other package. Verified via a from-scratch restore against nuget.org only
       (cleared sources, fresh package cache) — not just checking the version index. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseLocalHartsy)' != 'true'">
    <!-- Pin an EXACT version PUBLISHED on nuget.org. NEVER a "-local" suffix: those are locally-packed
         builds that live only on the dev machine's feed and are NOT on nuget.org, so a fresh clone fails
         restore with NU1301 (the "missing local dll" error). If a build hits NU1603, the version hasn't
         indexed yet — wait for the flat-container index, then rebuild with a cleared package cache.
         Verify at https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/hartsyinference/index.json
         (Note: the alpha.* line was reset to 2.0.0-alpha.N during the Engine-architecture rewrite; the
         alpha.49 note above predates that reset and refers to the old 1.0.0 line.)
         2.0.0-alpha.3 = latest published on nuget.org as of 2026-07-25; predates the same-day commit
         that made TextService.FindMmproj public (see SwarmUI-LLMAssistant's HartsyLocalLLMProvider).
         Pinned here to 2.0.0-alpha.4 in step with that fix and the other two SwarmUI-Hartsy extensions,
         ahead of the engine repo's manual publish of that version — restore will 404 until that push
         lands on nuget.org.
         2.0.0-alpha.10 = AudioModelCache.GetRepoDirectory/GetAsync/Get now take a required category
         parameter (tts/stt/music/clone/fx/misc) and default their cache root to
         {HARTSYINFERENCE_MODELS}/audio — AudioEngineBridge.GetWeightLocations's HF-auto-download branch
         requires this exact signature. Pinned ahead of publish, same as the alpha.4 precedent above.
         2.0.0-alpha.16 = first published version with MusicRequest.TopP/RepetitionPenalty, which
         AudioEngineRequests.cs has referenced since the YuE sampling pass. The pin sat at alpha.11 in the
         meantime, so ANY SwarmUI-driven rebuild (which never passes UseLocalHartsy) failed with CS0117 and
         silently dropped the extension. alpha.15 does NOT contain these — do not pin below 16.
         2.0.0-alpha.17 = adds SpeechRequest/TtsJob SpeakerId/NormalizeLoudness/Temperature/
         WaveformTemperature/TopP/TopK/MaxTokens/Emotion/SpeakingRate/PitchStd, FxSeparateRequest
         Shifts/Overlap/Segment/Seed, FxEnhanceRequest Nfe/Solver, and TtsModelDescriptor.
         VoiceSelectsWeights — needed since the Demucs/Enhance/Piper-voice work landed.
         2.0.0-alpha.18 = adds ISpeechService.SynthesizeStreamAsync + the public AudioChunk streaming
         surface (real incremental TTS — chunks arrive as the model generates them, not AudioLab's own
         text-chunk-and-regenerate loop), used by AudioEngineBridge.ProcessStreamAsync /
         DynamicAudioBackend.GenerateLiveNativeStreaming for the first `tts_streaming`-flagged model
         (Kyutai TTS). Packed to the local feed (`hartsy-local-nuget`) 2026-08-09; not yet pushed to
         nuget.org (that requires a git push in the read-only-git engine repo, left to the user).
         2.0.0-alpha.33 = kept in lockstep with SwarmUI-HartsyInference-Backend's pin; both extensions load
         their own copy of the engine, so a version split between them is a skew hazard, not an isolation win.
         2.0.0-alpha.47 = threads the CPU kernels. Piper's whole forward pass ran on one core, which is what
         made synthesis 89-96% of every voice turn on the CPU-only box this extension serves; measured 15x
         faster (real-time factor 3.1 -> 0.21) on an 8-core dev machine. Also brings HARTSY_CPU_THREADS.
         2.0.0-alpha.49 = end-of-speech stops counting the same silence twice, and the utterance wait uses the
         whole of UtteranceSeconds instead of UtteranceSeconds minus the lead-in. Without this the setting
         raised below buys 1.5 s less than it says: a question is cut off at UtteranceSeconds - 1.5.
         2.0.0-alpha.50 = Piper implements IStreamingTtsRunner by splitting on sentence boundaries, which is
         what /AudioLab/SpeakStream needs to send audio before the whole reply has been synthesized. Measured
         on the audit's four-sentence passage: first sentence at 1.797s against 8.315s for the whole thing.
         2.0.0-alpha.51 = status frames on the wake socket, so a satellite's light can say what the server is
         doing during the seconds it is otherwise silent.
         2.0.0-alpha.52 = BackendFactory.ProbeCuda / ResolveProbed. Resolving "auto" from whether a GPU is
         VISIBLE picks a card that may not work: kernels for another architecture, no free memory, a driver and
         toolkit that disagree all report available and then throw on the first real operation, in the middle
         of a user's request. The probe runs one matmul and checks the answer instead.          2.0.0-alpha.53 = WakeFrameCodec can write a payload after a header, and WakeService.SendAudioAsync
         paces a spoken reply over the wake socket. Needed by the turn orchestrator; also what makes the
         status frames reachable from an endpoint here.

         2.0.0-alpha.54 = WakeServiceOptions.HostHandlesTurns marks the transcript frame `handled`, which is the
         only thing that stops a satellite answering a turn the server is already answering; and
         WakeService.BeginAudio streams one reply across the several pieces synthesis produces it in, instead of
         one send per sentence that the device reads as one reply per sentence.

         2.0.0-alpha.55 = a satellite may declare "width": 1 in its hello and send G.711 u-law, one byte a
         sample. Halving the bytes is not about the link's average speed — it doubles how long a fixed send
         buffer rides out a stall, and a satellite on a marginal link loses audio in stalls, not to slowness.
         Needed here so the wake listener accepts what the firmware now offers; without it the server refuses
         the hello and the device falls back to 16-bit.

         2.0.0-alpha.67 = YuE2. The catalog entry, the `Yue2*` fields on MusicRequest (planning mode, a score to
         render verbatim, the score planner's own sampler block, penalty window, min tokens) and the audio
         endpoints finally serialising AudioResult.Meta, which is where YuE2 reports that a song hit its token
         budget before it ended. Without the bump the provider here compiles against a MusicRequest that has no
         YuE2 fields at all.

         2.0.0-alpha.68 = YuE2's acoustic pass stops using the decode-tuned attention kernel. It is prefill
         shaped — every frame of the song is a query row — and the general entry reaches cuDNN's fused engine,
         which is 26x on an op that was ~89% of that stack. A 3.9-minute song generates in 119 s instead of
         17.8 minutes, which is the difference between this model being usable here and not.

         2.0.0-alpha.70 = YuE2's semantic pass projects and samples only the 32,769-id window it can actually
         draw from instead of the whole 184,704-wide vocabulary, which is a 5.6x cut to the per-token head GEMM,
         the logit read-back and the host-side sampling scans. The same song generates in 109 s, which puts it
         ahead of the reference implementation's own rate on identical lyrics.

         2.0.0-alpha.73 = causal PREFILL stops running on the decode-tuned attention kernel and takes cuDNN's
         fused engine instead (6.8x on that op), and YuE2's acoustic attention block runs at its buffers' dtype
         end to end once ApplyRopeSingle accepts an F16 activation. Together a 3.9-minute song generates in
         97s instead of 107s. The prefill half of that is shared code, so every long prompt in the engine
         benefits, not only music.

         2.0.0-alpha.74 = REQUIRED by this extension's source, not just an upgrade: Score Penalty Window maps to
         MusicRequest.Yue2AbcPenaltyWindow, which does not exist before alpha.74, and Max Duration's 900 s
         ceiling is inert against an engine that clamps at 360. It also carries the budget clamp behind
         meta.budgetSeconds, which ReportTruncation reads to tell "the prompt ate the context" apart from "the
         song ran long". Verified through SwarmUI: 431 s of continuous music, and a 900 s request against a
         large score correctly reports 718.8 s granted instead of failing.

         2.0.0-alpha.79 = REQUIRED by this extension's source: the Score tab's Draft plan calls
         IMusicService.PlanScoreAsync/BudgetAsync, which do not exist before alpha.78. YuE2 writes its ABC score
         in a first pass and renders it in a second, so planning alone returns the editable artifact in seconds
         where a full render takes minutes - measured 14 s to plan a 25 s request against 13 s to render that
         same score back. alpha.82 = REQUIRED by this extension's source: the Score tab's Transcribe reads a recording
         into a score through ITranscribeService.RunScoreAsync, which does not exist before alpha.82. It returns
         both renderings of the score from one decode, because the decode is the whole cost and stripping the
         chord symbols out of the full rendering does NOT spell the melody rendering - a bar carrying a chord
         cannot fold into a multi-bar rest, and a chord change inside a held note splits it into tied parts.

         2.0.0-alpha.130 = catch-up bump, 2026-09-19 (alpha.74 -> alpha.130), merged with the Score tab's
         alpha.79/alpha.82 prerequisites above already satisfied by the newer pin. Keeps this extension in
         lockstep with SwarmUI-HartsyInference-Backend and SwarmUI-LLMAssistant.

         2.0.0-alpha.160 = the HeartMuLa and MiniMax Music 3 quant caches are read back under their source
         shapes, 2026-09-23. Every :q8/:q4 HeartLib and MiniMax variant on the CUDA path had failed with
         CUDA_ERROR_INVALID_VALUE since alpha.130: the engine's GGUF writer emits ggml axis order and the two
         cache readers took the file's order verbatim, so every projection was transposed and the GEMV's M came
         out 0. Existing cache files stay valid. Nothing between alpha.130 and alpha.160 needed a code change
         here: a full Release build from the nuget.org packages (UseLocalHartsy=false) succeeds as-is.

         2.0.0-alpha.235 = catch-up bump for the phone-call voice agent work, 2026-10-01 (alpha.183 -> alpha.235,
         52 versions). Nothing in that span changes a signature this extension calls: the eviction fix (alpha.218,
         #191), Kokoro's sentence-streaming runner (alpha.219, #194) and the Whisper/Kokoro speed and log-mel/GELU
         fidelity passes (alpha.224/227/232/233/235, #199/#204/#212/#211/#214) are all internal to the engine or
         additive; the new ISpeechService.OpenSynthesizerAsync/ITranscribeService.OpenTranscriberAsync leases
         (alpha.223, #201) default-interface-throw for callers that don't opt in. Verified: a full Release build
         from the nuget.org packages (UseLocalHartsy=false) succeeds as-is — see this PR for what actually
         changed in this extension (tts_streaming on Kokoro, resident leases, RNNoise installer wiring), landed as
         separate follow-up PRs on top of this pin so the bump itself stays reviewable on its own.

         2.0.0-alpha.237 = catch-up bump, 2026-10-01 (alpha.235 -> alpha.237). alpha.236 (#215) fixes
         SamplerChain/TopPStep/TopKStep/MinPStep to reuse scratch buffers instead of argsorting the whole
         vocabulary through a Comparison<int> delegate every non-greedy decode step — faster decode,
         token-identical output, no public signature touched. This IS on an AudioLab code path: this extension
         calls no ITextService/LLM-provider API directly, but AceStepLmPlanner.cs (ACE-Step's music-codes
         planner, Models/Music/) constructs HartsyInference.LLM.Sampling.SamplerChain directly
         (AceStepLmPlanner.cs:75,125) from the caller's Temperature/TopK/TopP, the exact sampler classes
         alpha.236 fixed — a grep of src/HartsyInference.Audio on the engine side found no other reference to
         these sampler types. ACE-Step runs non-greedy in normal use (Temperature > 0; greedy would hurt
         generation quality), so its planner gets the same faster, token-identical decode the voice/LLM path
         does. alpha.237 itself adds the new, separate HartsyInference.Voice package, which this extension now
         does reference, added just below by the voice-agent-session PR this comment was merged with:
         VoiceAgentSession/VoiceModelSet/VoiceAgentOptions for AudioServices/Voice/*, the browser "Voice Agent"
         tab.

         2.0.0-alpha.238 = the voice-agent-session PR's own follow-up bump, 2026-10-01 (alpha.237 -> alpha.238).
         Adds HartsyInference.Voice's VoiceAgentSession.ReadOutbound(Span<float>, out int turnId) overload,
         the one the phone host's own sender uses (VoiceHost/Calls/VoiceCall.cs) and this extension's outbound
         pump now uses too, tagging every sent frame with the turn that actually produced it instead of the
         StateChanged-event approximation the first version of this PR shipped before this overload was
         published. Otherwise only adds the VoiceHost exe and its deploy files, which this extension does not
         reference. Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on nuget.org at this
         version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.241 = 2026-10-02 (alpha.238 -> alpha.241). Dia's decode self-attention is GPU-resident (warm
         generation about 3.6x faster, bit-identical output); HeartMuLa's quantized GGUF cache resolves under
         modelsRoot/audio instead of ~/.cache; a bare `piper` request falls back to Piper's default voice instead
         of 404ing on piper.onnx (alpha.240); the TTS samplers stop sorting the whole vocabulary every token
         (Kyutai TTS -63 %, FishSpeech -37 %; alpha.239); ApplyRope's combined q+k overload is safe for GQA; LLM
         uploads fail fast on a retired CUDA stream. Voice gains opt-in prefix-KV reuse
         (TextRequest.PrefixCacheKey). Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on
         nuget.org at this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.242 = 2026-10-02 (alpha.241 -> alpha.242). A switch between audio models sizes the incoming
         model and evicts the others when free VRAM is under what it needs, instead of only under a fixed 3 GiB, and
         an out-of-VRAM failure unloads every other unpinned audio model and retries once inside the engine (Dia no
         longer fails and Orpheus no longer streams its weights after a long sweep; floor knob
         vram.audioEvictFreeVramFloorMb, default 3072). cuDNN skips nondeterministic engines by default, so
         Chatterbox, CosyVoice 2 and Piper repeat their output on identical calls; a short Dia prompt fails in seconds;
         Kokoro and the synthesizer lease stop a cancelled synthesis at the next stage; a cancelled LLM prefill frees
         the GPU within about two layers; prefix-KV reuse is bounded; the wake listener gains a per-device
         Claim/Release handoff. Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on nuget.org at
         this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.243 = 2026-10-02 (alpha.242 -> alpha.243). Carries HartsyInference#243: every CUDA op runs in
         its own backend's context, whatever another engine copy left bound on the thread. SwarmUI loads one private
         engine copy per extension, all on one thread pool, and each copy cached "this thread is bound to my
         context" for itself. AudioLab's Whisper on the 4090 trusted that cache on a thread LLMAssistant's copy had
         bound to the 3060, so its device calls went to the 3060's context. The sticky CUDA 700 that followed also
         took down the image backend's generations on the 4090 until a restart. The fix only protects the process
         when every engine copy in it has it, so LLMAssistant takes alpha.243 at the same time. Verified
         hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on nuget.org at this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.244 = 2026-10-02 (alpha.243 -> alpha.244). REQUIRED by this extension's source: carries
         HartsyInference#245 (AuK / AuK-Flash), which adds SpeechRequest.Instruction/DurationSeconds that
         AudioEngineRequests.Speech sets unconditionally (PR #45, auk_tts provider) — this extension does not
         compile against alpha.243. Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on
         nuget.org at this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.245 = 2026-10-02 (alpha.244 -> alpha.245). Carries HartsyInference#248: AuK only frees and
         reloads every stage's weights between calls when the device doesn't actually have room to keep them
         resident, instead of always evicting. Cuts the fixed per-call overhead the previous version paid on every
         AuK/AuK-Flash generation regardless of free VRAM. Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are
         both indexed on nuget.org at this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.246 = 2026-10-03 (alpha.245 -> alpha.246). REQUIRED by this extension's source: carries
         HartsyInference#249 (IndexTTS-1.5, zero-shot voice cloning), which adds the "indextts" Engine catalog
         entry IndexTtsProvider.cs/AudioEngineBridge's new indextts_tts binding resolve to — this extension does
         not compile against alpha.245 (ModelSpec.Requested = "indextts:v1_5" has nothing to resolve to before
         this version). Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on nuget.org at this
         version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.247 = 2026-10-04 (alpha.246 -> alpha.247). Carries HartsyInference#250: IndexTtsPipeline's
         ToF32 helper was unconditionally casting every tensor in the loaded weight dictionaries to F32, which
         crashed loading the real gpt.pth checkpoint's non-floating-point buffers (GPT-2-style integer position
         ids / boolean causal mask) with "Unsupported dtype conversion: I64 -> F32." — found via a real deploy of
         alpha.246 on swarm.hartsy.ai (AudioLabInstallEngine + GenerateText2Image against the real
         IndexTeam/IndexTTS-1.5 checkpoint), not by this extension's own code. No source change needed here; this
         bump exists only so a fresh install pulls the fixed engine. Verified hartsyinference and
         hartsyinference.voice are both indexed on nuget.org at this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.248 = 2026-10-04 (alpha.247 -> alpha.248). Carries HartsyInference#251: an audit of the
         IndexTTS-1.5 port against the real index-tts Python source found it was missing top-p (nucleus) sampling
         (only top-k + temperature were applied) and the reference's unconditional 20ms tail fade-out (stochastic
         stop-token sampling can land early, leaving an audible click at the end of a clip). Both fixed in the
         engine; no source change needed here. Verified hartsyinference and hartsyinference.voice are both indexed
         on nuget.org at this version before bumping.
         2.0.0-alpha.249 = 2026-10-04 (alpha.248 -> alpha.249). Carries HartsyInference#252: Kokoro output now
         matches the official model. The F0/energy predictor read the wrong features (pitch off by ~74 Hz), the
         voice-pack style row was two rows late, the English G2P is now a misaki port (Kokoro's training
